    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a clonal malignancy of bone marrow progenitors that are arrested in their ability to mature, and for which clinical outcomes have not improved in over 30 years. Mutation in DNA methyltransferase 3A (DNMT3A), an enzyme responsible for catalyzing de novo methylation at CpG sites throughout the genome, is present in approximately 20-25% of normal karyotype AML cases, with more than half of all mutations altering R882 within the catalytic domain. We have recently shown that DNMT3A R882H occurs frequently in mutated but otherwise normal preleukemic hematopoietic stem cells, but little is known regarding its mechanism of action in contributing to leukemogenesis in human cells. Our studies thus focus on characterizing the biological phenotypes associated with DNMT3A R882H in AML cell lines and cord blood-derived hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs) with regard to proliferation, cell survival, immunophenotype, and differentiation. Interestingly, we found that the R882H mutation causes a time-dependent reduction in mature myeloid antigen expression and decreased apoptosis in THP-1 cells, with minimal effects on cell proliferation, mitochondrial mass, and ATRA-induced myeloid differentiation. Lentiviral transduction of DNMT3A R882H into FACS-purified human CD34+ stem and progenitor cells also resulted in increases in both the Lin-CD34+ percentage and fraction of CD34+CD38- HSPCs, consistent with a role for the mutant in retaining stem cell character. In addition, we found that mutant DNMT3A conferred time-dependent and reversible growth factor independence in TF-1 cells. Transcriptional profiling of these cells revealed upregulation of genes including ANGPT1 and CD36, which are also expressed in primary DNMT3A-mutated AML samples from The Cancer Genome Atlas Network (TCGA). These findings provide evidence for the role of mutant DNMT3A in maintaining a primitive cell state, enhancing cell survival, and promoting growth factor independence, and together offer insight into the mechanisms by which the mutation contributes to leukemogenesis.",
